a("Enoch AI dating Dead Sea Scrolls", ent="enoch-dss-dating",
  mt="ENOCH ON THE OVERVIEW — GRONINGEN, 24 RADIOCARBON SAMPLES, BAYESIAN RIDGE REGRESSION, THE THIRTY-YEAR ERROR, 79% OF 135, ALL HELD; 'COPIES' KEPT APART FROM THEIR AUTHORS; 'OFTEN OLDER' MADE 'A SIGNIFICANT PORTION … 50 TO 150 YEARS', AND THE RADIOCARBON DANIEL CREDITED TO THE MODEL",
  d="ENOCH, BY THE OPERATOR'S ADDRESS 'ENOCH AI DATING DEAD SEA SCROLLS', ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-31: 'Researchers at the University of Groningen developed an artificial intelligence model named Enoch to re-date the Dead Sea Scrolls.' Training data, method, accuracy, findings (older timelines, 4Q114 Daniel and 4Q109 Ecclesiastes), the reaction (supporters, Reddit sceptics), a closing quotation of Popović from Haaretz. Nine cards: the PLOS article, the BAS, Reddit, the Jerusalem Post, Haaretz, the NIH copy of the article, a National Geographic post, two videos.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview says who built the model, on what, by what method, how accurate it is and what it found, then gives the reception, from the article and its press.",
  findings=["THE STUDY HELD. 'The model was trained on 24 radiocarbon-dated scroll samples', 'Bayesian ridge regression', 'a mean absolute error of roughly 28 to 31 years', '79% of its estimates for 135 undated scrolls aligned realistically with human paleographic evaluations': the record's 24 samples, its 27.9 to 30.7 years, its 135 scrolls and its 79%. " + CK,
            "COPIES KEPT APART FROM COMPOSITIONS. 'Some copies appear closer in time to their presumed historical authors'. The record's negative tag is that the model dates copies; the composition keeps the distinction in its own words. This is the distinction the field turns on.",
            "THE FINDING ENLARGED. 'Enoch indicates that a significant portion of the scrolls are 50 to 150 years older than traditional paleographic estimates.' The study: 'often older than traditionally assumed palaeographic estimates'. The range and the 'significant portion' are not in the saved sources.",
            "TWO RESULTS MADE ONE. 'The Daniel fragment (`4Q114`) and Ecclesiastes manuscript (`4Q109`) were backdated substantially'. The study separates them: 'this study’s 14 C result for 4Q114 and Enoch’s date prediction for 4Q109 now establish these to be the first known fragments of a biblical book from the time of their presumed authors'. The Daniel date is the radiocarbon result (230–160 BCE); only Ecclesiastes is the model's. The figures 'from 175–150 BCE to roughly 300–250 BCE' are not in the saved sources, which give Enoch's 4Q109 prediction as 'a third-century BCE date'.",
            "THE STATED LIMIT, ELSEWHERE. The study names its problem, 'an almost complete lack of date-bearing manuscripts'. The composition gives doubt through Reddit ('carbon-dating uncertainties') and Popović's caution through Haaretz; the study's own limit is unsaid."],
  analysis="A machine-dating study is composed nearly at its own grain, and it carries the distinction between when a text was copied and when it was written. Its figures grow where the press rounded them, and its doubts are sourced to a forum.",
  per_v={"author": True, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="Popović named in a quotation; Groningen named; no DOI; the PLOS article and its NIH copy cited.")

